  "response": "Researchers have used PISA and TIMSS data to compare the size of the mathematics gender gap across many countries. Their reasoning is that a difference fixed by biology should appear with roughly constant magnitude wherever it is measured, whereas a difference produced by socialization should rise and fall with the social arrangements of each society. The two programs supply this comparative leverage through different designs. PISA is administered by the International Association for the Evaluation of Educational Achievement, and because of that institutional arrangement, PISA assesses students who are about 15 years old, sampling them by age rather than by year of schooling. This age-based assessment of fifteen-year-olds came chronologically earlier than the grade-based design under which TIMSS assesses students in fourth and eighth grade.\n\nThe most widely cited use of these data is economic rather than psychological in origin. Guiso, Monte, Sapienza, and Zingales analyzed PISA 2003 data in a 2008 article titled \"Culture, Gender, and Math.\" The authors correlated national gaps with composite indices of women's economic participation, political representation, and educational attainment, along with measures of cultural attitudes toward women's roles. Their publication of \"Culture, Gender, and Math\" in the journal Nature confirms what the study established. Guiso and colleagues reported that the mathematics gender gap was smaller in countries with greater gender equality. That pattern made the size of the gap appear to track institutions and norms rather than a stable cognitive endowment.\n\nIceland became the emblematic case in this literature, although the national figures have been reported inconsistently. Either girls in Iceland outscored boys in mathematics in PISA 2003, or girls in Iceland did not outscore boys in mathematics in PISA 2003; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The OECD's official PISA 2003 report settled the matter, determining that girls in Iceland did outscore boys in mathematics in that assessment. Under the second possibility, Iceland would supply no reversal of the usual pattern. Researchers have treated large cross-national variation in the math gender gap as evidence against a primarily innate explanation, and the association between gender equality and a narrower gap that Guiso and colleagues documented indicates as much. On this view, the variation itself carries the argument, whatever the outcome in any single country.\n\nA second strand of research asks how large the gap is once many national samples are pooled. Else-Quest, Hyde, and Linn found that average gender differences in mathematics across countries were very small. In other words, the 2010 meta-analysis by Else-Quest, Hyde, and Linn reported near-zero mean effect sizes for gender differences in mathematics. Their analysis aggregated results from PISA and TIMSS across dozens of participating nations. It treated the small pooled mean and the substantial dispersion around it as jointly informative. A negligible average combined with marked national heterogeneity is difficult to reconcile with a universal cognitive cause.\n\nThese findings have not produced a single-factor account. At least one of two contributions must be present, perhaps both: sociocultural factors contribute to the gender gap in mathematics performance, or biological factors contribute to the gender gap in mathematics performance. The strongest challenge to a purely sociocultural account focused on the upper tail rather than the mean. Proponents of the greater male variability hypothesis argued that innate differences in variance cause males to be overrepresented among top mathematics scorers. Despite that argument, the case for a sociocultural contribution has held, because the evidence on variance favors it. Kane and Mertz concluded that the cross-national data did not support the greater male variability hypothesis. They showed that variance ratios, like mean differences, fluctuated widely between countries and in some nations favored girls. Whereas a biological contribution to the gap remains open as a matter of principle, that cross-national evidence on variance removed the most prominent empirical basis for treating innate variability as the decisive cause of male overrepresentation at the top.",
